## Error Handling

- Use structured exception handling
- Log errors with appropriate context
- Fail fast for unrecoverable errors
- Return meaningful error messages

## Testing

- Write unit tests for business logic
- Use descriptive test names
- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert pattern
- Aim for meaningful coverage, not 100%

## Git Conventions

### Branch Naming

```
feature/[description]    # New features
bugfix/[description]     # Bug fixes
hotfix/[description]     # Urgent production fixes
refactor/[description]   # Code refactoring
```

### Commit Messages

```
[type]: Brief description

- Detail 1
- Detail 2

{{WORK_ITEM_PREFIX}}XXXX
```

Types: `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `docs`, `test`, `chore`

### Version Bumping

**Always bump VERSION after adding features or making changes:**

| Change Type | Version Bump | Example |
|-------------|--------------|---------|
| New feature | Minor (x.Y.0) | 1.4.0 → 1.5.0 |
| Bug fix | Patch (x.y.Z) | 1.5.0 → 1.5.1 |
| Breaking change | Major (X.0.0) | 1.5.0 → 2.0.0 |

Include version in commit message: `Bump version to X.Y.Z - description`

## Documentation

- Document public APIs
- Keep README up to date
- Use inline comments sparingly (code should be self-explanatory)
- Update `.ai/memory/` files for AI context

